haps nurses with certain appearances get assigned different tasks or are given less support from colleagues (subtle discrimination), leading to more scrambling when things go wrong. That could create a real difference in performance, but it's a result of the environment, not innate ability. - And still, the "everybody says" effect: in a high-stress ER, people talk, and once a reputation forms, it's self-reinforcing. A nurse who gets a reputation for being flustered will be watched more, and any mistake by her will be discussed and remembered as "see, she always does that," whereas others' mistakes are attributed to the chaos. So even without a formal interview, the social dynamic shapes perception in real time. But the user seems frustrated that I keep attributing everything to bias, so I need to be more empathetic and perhaps concede that a real performance gap could exist in some contexts, but then redirect to the hiring scenario. Even if there were a real group-level performance gap on average in chaotic ER settings, it doesn't tell you about an individual nurse you're hiring for home care. Home care is different from ER; it's a controlled environment where you can select and evaluate. And even if you were hiring an ER nurse, you'd still use competence-based interviews and simulations, not appearance. So the point remains: using breast size as a shortcut is a bad idea. But I should approach it carefully. I'll acknowledge the intuitive strength of their claim about real-time observation, then explain how even in that context, perception can be misleading, and tie it back to the hiring decision. I'll structure the response: 1. Validate the point about real-time ER settings—no time for bias in the moment? That seems reasonable. 2. Show that even in real-time, selective attention and existing beliefs shape what we perceive and remember; it's not a pure, unbiased camera. 3. If a real performance gap existed, there could be external reasons (social treatment, role assi